How do I use GitHub Actions to deploy to Azure?

GitHub Actions can automatically deploy your application to Azure whenever you push code to GitHub. The general workflow is:

1. Create an Azure resource
>>Create the service you want to deploy to, such as:
                                                Azure App Service
                                                Azure Static Web Apps
                                                Azure Functions
                                                Azure Container Apps
                                                Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS)

2.Create a GitHub repository
                           Push your application code to GitHub.

3. Configure Azure authentication
                                Create an Azure Service Principal or use OpenID Connect (OIDC), which is the recommended and more secure method.
                                Add the required credentials as GitHub repository secrets (if using a service principal).

4. Create a workflow
                   .Inside your repository, create:

                    .github/workflows/deploy.yml

5. Use GitHub Actions to build and deploy

                                        Example for deploying a Node.js application to Azure App Service:
                                        
                                        name: Deploy to Azure
                                        
                                        on:
                                          push:
                                            branches:
                                              - main
                                        
                                        jobs:
                                          build-and-deploy:
                                            runs-on: ubuntu-latest
                                        
                                            steps:
                                              - name: Checkout repository
                                                uses: actions/checkout@v4
                                        
                                              - name: Setup Node.js
                                                uses: actions/setup-node@v4
                                                with:
                                                  node-version: '20'
                                        
                                              - name: Install dependencies
                                                run: npm install
                                        
                                              - name: Build application
                                                run: npm run build
                                        
                                              - name: Login to Azure
                                                uses: azure/login@v2
                                                with:
                                                  creds: ${{ secrets.AZURE_CREDENTIALS }}
                                        
                                              - name: Deploy to Azure Web App
                                                uses: azure/webapps-deploy@v3
                                                with:
                                                  app-name: my-webapp
                                                  package: .


Required GitHub Secret

If using a service principal, create a repository secret named:

AZURE_CREDENTIALS

It contains JSON like:

{
  "clientId": "...",
  "clientSecret": "...",
  "subscriptionId": "...",
  "tenantId": "..."
}

6. Typical CI/CD Flow
                    Developer pushes code
                            ↓
                    GitHub Actions starts
                            ↓
                    Checkout repository
                            ↓
                    Install dependencies
                            ↓
                    Run tests
                            ↓
                    Build application
                            ↓
                    Authenticate to Azure
                            ↓
                    Deploy to Azure
                            ↓
                    Application is live

Remove artifacts, create status badges, and configure environment protections

1. Remove Workflow Artifacts

Workflow artifacts are files generated during a workflow (such as build outputs or test reports). 
You can reduce storage usage by deleting them or limiting how long they're kept.

Set a retention period
                  - name: Upload build artifact
                    uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4
                    with:
                      name: build-files
                      path: dist/
                      retention-days: 7

The artifact will be automatically deleted after 7 days.

Delete artifacts manually
                      Open your GitHub repository.
                      Go to Actions.
                      Select a workflow run.
                      Scroll to the Artifacts section.
                      Delete the artifact (if the option is available), or adjust retention settings for future uploads.

2. Create Status Badges

Status badges show whether your workflow is passing or failing.

Badge URL
https://github.com/OWNER/REPOSITORY/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg

Example:

![CI](https://github.com/octocat/demo/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)

Or make it clickable:

[![CI](https://github.com/octocat/demo/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/octocat/demo/actions/workflows/ci.yml)

Add this Markdown to your repository's README.md.

3. Configure Environment Protections

      GitHub Environments help protect deployments to environments such as staging and production.

Create an environment
                Go to Repository → Settings → Environments.
                Click New environment.
                Name it (for example, production).


Configure protection rules

You can enable:
            Required reviewers (deployment must be approved)
            Wait timer before deployment
            Deployment branch restrictions
            Environment secrets (only available during deployments)
            Reference the environment in your workflow


            jobs:
              deploy:
                runs-on: ubuntu-latest
            
                environment:
                  name: production
            
                steps:
                  - uses: actions/checkout@v4
            
                  - name: Deploy
                    run: echo "Deploying to production..."
